## Decision order

1. explicit user provider requirement;
2. security and data-classification eligibility;
3. required tool and capability availability;
4. provider health and capacity;
5. independent-review requirement;
6. benchmarked task-fit evidence;
7. cost and latency among equally qualified choices;
8. configured default only when earlier factors are neutral.

Policy always overrides preference. The current router is rule-based and its existing Claude-first/code-affinity defaults remain an unevaluated contract baseline. `model-policy.yml` prohibits a learned router and accepts no tuning until repeated live results meet quality and security guardrails.

Deterministic preflight performs normal context inventory, estimation, deduplication, and stable-prefix compilation. Token-auditor is conditional: budget breach, significant duplication, routing ambiguity, unusual context growth, or capacity/fallback signal only.

Exact consumer-subscription allowance is not available reliably, so the system never fabricates tokens remaining.

## PR-0.6 observation boundary

The Section 29B.2 Task Classifier now runs once for each WorkUnit created by the orchestration simulation. It reads the WorkUnit's fixed execution role, risk tier, permission level, independent-review requirement, context size, and write requirement, then records one private `TaskClassified` event. The event can be joined to an `AgentRun` by `workUnitId` to compare the predicted task class with the provider that actually executed it.

Classification remains log-only. Neither the task-class label nor its comparison result is passed to `ProviderRouter`, `CapacityManager`, `Broker`, or a provider adapter. The routable provider catalog and every role's eligibility list remain Claude/Codex-only.

`HarnessAdapter` is an unregistered `ProviderAdapter` implementation for OpenCode with a Groq backend. It compiles the same provider-neutral WorkRequest/context artifact used at the existing adapter boundary, invokes `opencode run --format json --model groq/<model>` with `GROQ_API_KEY` injected into the child environment, captures process output, and normalizes OpenCode NDJSON into the shared ProviderRun/AgentRun telemetry path. Groq is accepted only as an adapter execution identity so telemetry can name the real backend; it is not present in the broker's live provider states or routing table. No live Beacon WorkUnit or eval evidence is claimed.
